<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petru Aron</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1469">1469</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petru Aron was Prince of Moldavia three times between 1451 and 1457, during the civil wars that followed the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-good">Alexander the Good</a>. He seized power by killing Prince Bogdan II, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a>, and in 1456 became the first Moldavian ruler to agree to pay tribute to the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. Stephen overthrew him in 1457 and later had him executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petru-aron.jpg" alt="Petru Aron" width="187" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petru_Aron.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Petru Aron, an illegitimate son of Alexandru cel Bun, ruled Moldavia from October 1451 to February 1452, from August 1454 to February 1455, and from May 1455 to April 1457; the first two reigns fell during a civil war with Alexăndrel. He came to the throne by assassinating Bogdan II while Bogdan was at a wedding in Rauseni, and he later forced Alexăndrel to take refuge in the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhorod-dnistrovskyi">Cetatea Albă</a>.</p><p>He confirmed the commercial privileges his father had granted to Polish traders in Moldavia and took an oath of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV of Poland</a>. In 1456, to secure his southern borders, he agreed to pay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> a tribute of 2,000 gold ducats. He also issued new billon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins on the grosh design but struck in better <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, probably as part of a reform of monetary standards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The real challenge came from Bogdan II's son, Ștefan cel Mare, who had been protected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a>, captain-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, and, after Hunyadi's death, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlad-the-impaler">Vlad the Impaler</a>, the new Prince of Wallachia; on one reading of the Chronicle of Putna, Vlad offered Ștefan his full support. In April 1457, after two battles, Petru Aron was deposed and fled, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and then to the Székely Land, where he helped to raise tensions between Ștefan and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. He was later captured and executed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter Aaron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
